{"command": "cd /testbed && python3 - <<'PYEOF'\np=\"specs/001-transactions/spec.md\"\ns=open(p).read()\nanchor=\"### Edge Cases\"\nnew_stories=\"\"\"### User Story 4 - Bulk invalidation with any/all semantics (Priority: P2)\n\nA developer removes many entries at once by several tags, choosing whether\nan entry must carry *any* of the tags or *all* of them, and gets back the\nnumber of distinct entries removed.\n\n**Why this priority**: Bulk tag operations are the main scaling path for\ntag-based cleanup; they build directly on the P1/P2 tag machinery.\n\n**Independent Test**: Seed entries with overlapping tag sets, call the\nbulk-invalidation method in each mode, and verify exactly the expected\nentries are gone and the returned count equals the number of distinct\nremoved keys.\n\n**Acceptance Scenarios**:\n\n1. **Given** entries tagged {a}, {b}, and {a,b}, **When** bulk\n   invalidation is requested with tags (a, b) in mode `any`, **Then** all\n   three entries are removed and the return value is 3.\n2. **Given** the same entries, **When** bulk invalidation is requested\n   with tags (a, b) in mode `all`, **Then** only the entry tagged {a,b}\nis removed and the return value is 1.\n3. **Given** any cache state, **When** bulk invalidation is requested with\n   an empty tag collection (empty list, empty tuple, or exhausted\ngenerator), **Then** zero entries are removed in either mode and the\n   return value is 0 (explicitly NOT the old design-note behavior of\n   matching every key in `all` mode).\n4. **Given** a cache state, **When** bulk invalidation is requested with an\n   invalid mode (anything other than `any` or `all`) or invalid tags (bare\n   string, non-iterable, non-string element), **Then** `ValueError` is\n   raised and no entry is removed.\n5. **Given** a single-tag request, **When** bulk invalidation is called\n   with one tag, **Then** the result is identical to the single-tag\n   `invalidate(tag)` (compatibility preserved).\n\n---\n\n### User Story 5 - Resize capacity safely (Priority: P3)\n\nA developer changes the cache capacity at runtime and expects surviving\nentries to keep their order and state; a failed resize must change\nnothing.\n\n**Why this priority**: Runtime capacity tuning is a convenience/correctness\nguard rail; it depends on the P1 entry model and P2 LRU order.\n\n**Independent Test**: Create a cache, populate it, shrink and grow it,\nverify survivors, LRU order, and that an invalid resize leaves the cache\nbyte-identical.\n\n**Acceptance Scenarios**:\n\n1. **Given** a cache with capacity 5 and 3 entries, **When** it is resized\n   to 2, **Then** the two least-recently-used entries are evicted, the\n   remaining entry order is preserved, and the capacity now reports 2.\n2. **Given** a cache with capacity 2 and 2 entries, **When** it is resized\n   to 5, **Then** no entry is evicted and the capacity now reports 5.\n3. **Given** any cache state, **When** a resize is requested with an\n   invalid capacity (non-integer, boolean, zero, negative), **Then**\n   `ValueError` is raised and all entries, tags, recency order, and the\n   previous capacity are unchanged.\n4. **Given** a cache that has entries, **When** it is shrunk to a smaller\n   capacity, **Then** evicted entries' tag memberships are removed exactly\n   as with LRU eviction (no stale tags).\n5. **Given** a surviving entry across a resize, **When** the resize\n   completes, **Then** the entry's stored state is preserved as-is (no\n   re-copying, no expiry recomputation — see Assumptions for the expiry\n   clarification note).\n\n---\n\n\"\"\"\ns=s.replace(anchor,new_stories+anchor,1)\n# Add milestone-2 edge cases before '## Requirements'\nedge_add=\"\"\"- Bulk invalidation with duplicate tags in the request (deduplicated\n  before matching; count is distinct removed keys).\n- Bulk invalidation `all` mode with an empty tag set (removes zero,\n  never every key — supersedes the old design note).\n- Bulk invalidation with a generator tag source (consumed exactly once;\n  on validation failure the cache is unchanged).\n- Resize to the same capacity (no-op; state unchanged).\n- Resize shrinking to exactly the current entry count (no eviction).\n- Resize with boolean/zero/negative/non-int capacity (rejected, state\n  preserved).\n- Growing then shrinking back (order and entries survive the round trip).\n\"\"\"\nreq_anchor=\"## Requirements *(mandatory)*\"\ns=s.replace(req_anchor,edge_add+req_anchor,1)\nopen(p,\"w\").write(s)\nprint(\"stories inserted:\", s.count(\"### User Story\"), \"| edge block present:\", \"Bulk invalidation with duplicate tags\" in s)\nPYEOF", "exit_code": 0, "stderr": "", "stdout": "stories inserted: 5 | edge block present: True\n"}